1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,379
This is a Philips Videopac.

2
00:00:02,459 --> 00:00:06,022
In America, the Magnavox Odyssey². 1978.

3
00:00:06,102 --> 00:00:10,120
Everything made for it is here, in a
browser.

4
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:14,328
Underneath is webretro, running the open-
source O2EM core.

5
00:00:14,408 --> 00:00:18,708
Its Quick Menu restarts a game without
going back to the library.

6
00:00:18,788 --> 00:00:23,020
Leaving asks you to confirm — that's the
browser, not the Vault.

7
00:00:23,400 --> 00:00:25,453
Two hundred and thirteen cartridges.

8
00:00:25,533 --> 00:00:28,535
Box art for every one, the original manual
for most,

9
00:00:28,615 --> 00:00:30,787
a bit of history, and a gameplay clip.

10
00:00:30,867 --> 00:00:34,520
It all runs on your own machine — nothing
uploaded, no account.

11
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:40,170
The platform filter splits the original
G7000 from the G7400 —

12
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:44,420
the later machine, with the extra graphics
chip. Forty use it.

13
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,096
Then by where a cartridge came from.

14
00:00:47,176 --> 00:00:49,453
Nine Jopac titles, sold only in France.

15
00:00:49,533 --> 00:00:53,020
Sixteen Brazilian releases with their own
Portuguese names.

16
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:55,558
And the corners that never reached a shop:

17
00:00:55,638 --> 00:00:58,762
eighteen rare prototypes, twenty-three
modified carts

18
00:00:58,842 --> 00:01:01,180
where someone went back in to fix a bug,

19
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:04,565
and seventeen homebrew games still being
written today —

20
00:01:04,645 --> 00:01:06,620
forty years after Philips stopped.

21
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:12,363
You can also filter by what a game
actually is.

22
00:01:12,443 --> 00:01:15,953
Maze, shooter, sports, racing. One player
or two.

23
00:01:16,033 --> 00:01:19,616
The filters stack, so you can ask for a
two-player

24
00:01:19,696 --> 00:01:22,620
G7400 shooter — and find there isn't one.

25
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:26,150
Five languages. Not just the buttons —

26
00:01:26,230 --> 00:01:28,792
the setup instructions and control notes
too.

27
00:01:28,872 --> 00:01:32,020
Fitting, for a console sold under four
different names.

28
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,471
Star anything and it lands in your
favourites,

29
00:01:35,551 --> 00:01:36,914
remembered between visits.

30
00:01:36,994 --> 00:01:40,408
They live in the browser, not the folder,
so they don't travel.

31
00:01:40,488 --> 00:01:43,292
Export writes them to a small file; import
merges it

32
00:01:43,372 --> 00:01:46,620
into whatever is already starred, without
removing anything.

33
00:01:57,000 --> 00:01:58,934
Munchkin is the one with a story.

34
00:01:59,014 --> 00:02:02,718
Atari sued Philips, said it was too close
to Pac-Man, and won.

35
00:02:02,798 --> 00:02:04,610
It was pulled from the shelves.

36
00:02:04,690 --> 00:02:07,418
The K.C. stands for Kenneth C. Meinken
Junior,

37
00:02:07,498 --> 00:02:09,920
then president of North American Philips.

38
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:17,714
Every game lists its own controls.

39
00:02:17,794 --> 00:02:21,658
And some hide things: Munchkin has a maze
editor

40
00:02:21,738 --> 00:02:24,040
nowhere on the select screen.

41
00:02:24,120 --> 00:02:27,820
Press P, draw your own maze, play it. In
1981.

42
00:02:28,200 --> 00:02:31,903
The manual is here too. Scanned page by
page, the real thing —

43
00:02:31,983 --> 00:02:35,320
where you find out what the game
variations actually do.

44
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:03,104
Race was cartridge number one — three
games on it,

45
00:03:03,184 --> 00:03:04,887
sold as Speedway in America.

46
00:03:04,967 --> 00:03:08,198
Look at the controls. The console had two
joysticks,

47
00:03:08,278 --> 00:03:10,681
and each game picks which one it reads.

48
00:03:10,761 --> 00:03:14,820
Race only reads the second. That's why the
arrow keys do nothing.

49
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:46,920
More, then Restart. No reloading.

50
00:03:48,000 --> 00:03:51,461
This is why. Game two is a race against
someone else

51
00:03:51,541 --> 00:03:55,820
on one keyboard: one player on the arrows,
the other on W A S D.

52
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:02,762
Some cartridges survive in more than one
form.

53
00:04:02,842 --> 00:04:06,036
This is a twelve-K banked dump: it holds
more program

54
00:04:06,116 --> 00:04:08,322
than the console can address at once,

55
00:04:08,402 --> 00:04:10,793
so it switches between banks as it runs.

56
00:04:10,873 --> 00:04:13,820
The Vault tells you which copy you're
looking at.

57
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:23,185
One thing the manual has to tell you:

58
00:04:23,265 --> 00:04:25,328
Q — the fire button — changes gear.

59
00:04:25,408 --> 00:04:28,083
Nothing on screen suggests it, and without
it

60
00:04:28,163 --> 00:04:29,920
you'll never get out of first.

61
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,598
A few cartridges weren't only cartridges.

62
00:04:50,678 --> 00:04:54,713
The Master Strategy games came with a
board and playing pieces;

63
00:04:54,793 --> 00:04:56,868
Musician with a keyboard overlay.

64
00:04:56,948 --> 00:05:00,069
This list is where something else was in
the box.

65
00:05:00,149 --> 00:05:02,420
Scans of those are still being made.

66
00:05:06,000 --> 00:05:08,959
Setting up takes three steps. You need
Python first —

67
00:05:09,039 --> 00:05:11,712
it runs the small local server, because
browsers

68
00:05:11,792 --> 00:05:14,120
won't load an emulator off your hard disk.

69
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,809
When the catalogue improves, one button
fetches it.

70
00:05:20,889 --> 00:05:23,755
Two hundred and thirteen cartridges, and
forty years

71
00:05:23,835 --> 00:05:26,304
of a console most people have never heard
of.

72
00:05:26,384 --> 00:05:27,720
If you had one, you know.
